Assistant: Of course!  An if/then statement lets you do conditional execution in your code.  Here's an example:

    if (x > 0) {
        // do something
    } else if (x == 0) {
        // do something else
    } else {
        // do something else even when x is negative
    }

In this example, if x is greater than 0, then the code inside the first parenthese will be executed.  If x equals 0, then the code inside the first and third parentheses will be executed.  If x is negative, then the code inside the second and fourth parentheses will be executed.  You can have as many or as few if/then statements as you need.
